 from Code Itself",129,"2026-09-21"],["2608.09819","Macaron-V1: Towards Open Continual Learning with Self-Improvement and Mixture-of-LoRA",113,"2026-08-11"],["2609.20519","SoL-Pi: Recursively Scaling Auto-Research Loops for Efficient Agent Harness",112,"2026-09-18"],["2608.12307","AI4AI at Test-Time: Strong-to-Weak Capability Transfer via Harnesses",107,"2026-08-13"]],"featured":[{"id":"2611.12122","date":"2026-11-10","title":"System Optimizations for Cost-Efficient Agent Harnesses","tldr":"Agent costs are dominated by repeatedly re-reading context, and an interposition layer called Agency cuts costs by about 23% on average for existing harnesses with little accuracy loss."},{"id":"2611.10202","date":"2026-11-10","title":"Semiprime Factorization with Discrete Diffusion and Beam Search: Scaling Laws","tldr":"A discrete-diffusion transformer learns to factor semiprimes up to 68 bits, but scaling laws show compute grows exponentially with bit length, so RSA is not threatened."},{"id":"2611.06483","date":"2026-11-07","title":"What Does Multi-Turn Reinforcement Learning Really Improve in LLM Agents?","tldr":"Multi-turn RL helps LLM agents mainly by improving recovery from errors over long horizons, not single-step decisions, so its gains shrink when the turn budget is tight; mixing budgets in training narrows that gap."},{"id":"2611.05036","date":"2026-11-06","title":"ToolSpace: Scaling Tool-Centric Environment Synthesis for Long-Horizon Agents","tldr":"ToolSpace builds agent training tasks from how real tools are chained in execution traces, producing cross-service workflows that lift open models toward frontier tool-use scores."},{"id":"2611.01560","date":"2026-11-04","title":"Democratizing Robot-Use Agents: A Unified Harness with Open Models","tldr":"An open-weight Qwen model, driven through a carefully designed tool harness and with no privileged inputs, beats the published GPT-6 Astra result on the RoboDojo robot benchmark."},{"id":"2611.05266","date":"2026-11-04","title":"Stabilizing On-Policy Targeted Guidance in Agentic Reinforcement Learning","tldr":"Turning a judge's per-turn text feedback into a self-distillation signal, only on failing turns and alongside normal RL rewards, teaches coding agents behaviors that plain RL never discovers."},{"id":"2611.03633","date":"2026-11-04","title":"Pretraining Data Scale Reverses the Muon-AdamW Fine-Tuning Gap","tldr":"Muon beats AdamW at pretraining loss but, with enough pretraining data, AdamW models fine-tune better, and the crossover arrives sooner as models grow."},{"id":"2611.02270","date":"2026-11-03","title":"Hinted Scaling Laws","tldr":"Giving models partial reference solutions turns a hard benchmark into a smooth family of easier ones, letting scaling laws fit on small, near-zero-accuracy models forecast large-model benchmark accuracy with far lower error."},{"id":"2610.40362","date":"2026-11-01","title":"Your LR Schedule May Not Be Principled: Effective Learning Rate Scheduling in Weight-Normalized Training","tldr":"Weight normalization's pretraining gains over weight decay come mostly from its effective learning-rate trajectory; scheduling that quantity with a theory-derived inverse-square-root shape (AIR) cuts MoE loss about 0.01 below cosine."},{"id":"2610.33624","date":"2026-10-29","title":"RSI Arena: Towards Recursive Self-Improvement with Human Feedback","tldr":"Ten frontier models were each given six days to autonomously post-train the same 30B base model; the best, trained by GPT-6 Astra, tied NVIDIA's own instruct model in style-controlled human ratings."},{"id":"2610.33512","date":"2026-10-27","title":"Eat Your Precision: Precision Objective Mitigates Reward Hacking in Rubric-based RL","tldr":"Rubric rewards for open-ended RL only measure coverage, so models learn to pad; adding a precision term for necessary, factually correct sentences raises win rate over the base model from 10.6% to 68.0%."},{"id":"2610.31867","date":"2026-10-26","title":"Rewriting Scaling Laws for Synthetic Data with Compute and Data Constraints","tldr":"A scaling law that charges for the compute spent generating synthetic rewrites predicts how much of a pretraining corpus should be rewritten, and shows that models trained on rewrites can beat the rewriter."}]}